  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Sunday)\nIssued at 1112 AM MDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nWestern CONUS upper ridge will retrograde and flatten as a strong\nupper trough moves across interior Canada. However, the ridge \nwill rebuild and support hot weather by the end of the week, with \n90s and 100s returning. Strong upper trough continues to advance \nthrough the Northern Plains. A cool front has moved through the \nregion this morning with breezy north-northeast winds across the \nFA. However, the main cold front will push through the FA late \nthis afternoon and early evening, with a period of strong winds \nexpected, esp across the western SD Plains where gusts of 50 to 60\nmph will be possible for a short period per pressure rise \nforecast. The strongest winds will be in the lee of the Black \nHills, likely affecting KRAP. In addition, a batch of thick low \nlevel smoke from OR/WA wildfires will continue to push through the\nregion behind the front through tonight. The worst of the smoke \nwill move through late this morning into the early afternoon. \nConds will improve gradually overnight, but some lingering smoke \nis possible into Tues, esp across the far SW FA. Isolated \nthunderstorms will still be possible in scentral SD this evening \nalong the secondary front. In addition, could still could see a \nfew showers and TS across the scentral SD later tonight into Tue \nmorning as the jet entrance region will overlap with a weak \nimpulse passing to the south of the FA, supporting elevated \nconvection. Temps will fall into the 40s and 50s tonight with a \nfew 30s expected in portions of the higher Black Hills. Highs Tues\nwill be 10 to 15 degrees below seasonal norms mainly in the 70s, \nwith 60s in the Black Hills. Western ridge will gradually rebuild \nlate in the week. This will support a warm up with gradual \nincreasing LL moisture as SE return flow ensues. Weak pos theta-e \nadv aloft may support isolated showers and maybe a TS Wed night \nacross northeast WY and far SW SD. However, models have backed \noff on the northern extent and coverage of showers, adjusted pops\ndown. Also pulled pops over the BH Wed afternoon, as much drier \nBL conds are expected with weaker moisture advection into the \narea. Expect highs to be back in the 90s by Thur, with triple \ndigits possible by Friday and Sat on the SD Plains. Dry weather is\nexpected through the weekend. Flow looks to become a little more \nactive later next week as some of the longer range models \nindicate a stalled frontal boundary INVOF the FA with faster flow \naloft and appreciable LL moisture.",
